// WotLK ITEM GENERATOR - MANIFEST GUIDE
//
// This file intentionally uses JSON-with-comments. The generator strips // and
// /* ... */ comments before parsing, so these instructions can stay here.
// A strict JSON parser will reject comments; remove the comments first if you
// need to use this file in another tool.
// Do not add trailing commas: after comments are removed, the remaining text
// must still be ordinary JSON.
//
// QUICK START
// 1. Keep the source files beside generate_pack.py, or pass the source paths
//    supported by the CLI. Map/creature/encounter source files are validated.
// 2. Edit the active JSON below, or copy one of the examples after the JSON
//    into the matching profiles/recipes/quest_targets array.
// 3. Run from this directory:
//
//      py .\generate_pack.py --content-manifest .\content_manifest.example.json --seed 424242
//
//    Do not combine --content-manifest with --number or --class. Put all item
//    counts and class constraints in recipes instead.
// 4. The generator validates every recipe before writing SQL. It emits item
//    SQL, world loot, dungeon/raid loot, cleanup SQL, and placement reports.
//
// ACTIVE EXAMPLE
// The active example is deliberately small and runnable without a quest SQL
// source. It creates ten epic items for the example dungeon profile below.
// Set quest_targets to a non-empty list only after adding --quest-template-source.
//
// TOP-LEVEL FIELDS
// - version: must be 1.
// - profiles: explicit dungeon/raid profiles. Each profile has encounters.
// - recipes: exact item-generation requests. Counts are disjoint by default.
// - quest_targets: optional fixed/choice quest reward assignments.
//
// PROFILE FIELDS
// - id: unique stable profile ID used by recipes.profile.
// - instance: human-readable name shown in reports.
// - map_id/difficulty_id: source-validated map and difficulty identifiers.
// - map_type: 1 for dungeon, 2 for raid; used for placement reporting.
// - item_level_min/item_level_max: profile-wide item-level limits.
// - loot_mode: optional AzerothCore LootMode bit. If omitted for a mapped
//   difficulty, the generator uses 1 << difficulty_id.
// - additional_drop_chance: chance for the generated encounter pool to roll.
// - encounters: ordered by requires, not by JSON order or creature ID.
//
// ENCOUNTER FIELDS
// - id: unique inside the profile. Use "trash" for a trash group if desired.
// - kind: exactly "trash" or "boss".
// - requires: prerequisite encounter IDs; branches can share a rank.
// - weight: generated-item allocation weight. Bosses commonly use 3-5,
//   trash commonly uses 1.
// - item_level: optional two-value encounter range; it overrides the profile
//   range for that encounter.
// - quantity: generated item quantity per successful pool roll; default 1.
// - loot_mode: optional per-encounter override.
// - targets: exact source targets. creature entries are creature loot-table
//   Entry values; reference entries are reference_loot_template Entry values.
//   Add creature_entry when a creature's lootid differs from the target Entry.
//
// RECIPE FIELDS
// - id: unique recipe name.
// - count: exact number of ordinary items.
// - class or classes: one class, or a deterministic choice among listed classes.
// - required_level: independent player required-level range [min, max].
// - item_level: independent item-level range [min, max]. Omit it to use the
//   generator's normal required-level progression curve.
// - quality: 2 uncommon, 3 rare, 4 epic, 5 legendary.
// - kind: use "weapon" to restrict the recipe to weapons.
// - weapon_kind: optional value such as "1h_sword", "bow", or "staff".
// - role: optional role constraint such as tank, healer, caster, or melee.
// - profile: attach the recipe to a dungeon/raid profile.
// - target_kind: "general", "dungeon", "raid", or "quest".
// - target: optional encounter ID. Omit it to allocate across profile
//   encounters by their weights.
// - set_count/set_size: creates set_count complete sets, each set_size pieces.
//   For example, 20 Rogue sets with five pieces each means 100 generated items.
//
// RECIPE MIXING
// Every recipe contributes an exact, non-overlapping quota unless the future
// overlap option is explicitly enabled. A mixed manifest can therefore hold
// leveling gear, targeted raid gear, weapons, and sets together. The final
// item count is the sum of ordinary count values plus set_count * set_size.
//
// PLACEMENT OUTPUTS
// - world_item_placements.csv: one row per generated item, world pool/bracket,
//   exact world reference entries, covered world levels, and chance.
// - dungeon_raid_item_placements.csv: one row per placed item, instance,
//   difficulty, boss/trash encounter, LootMode, item-level band, and targets.
// - validation_report.json records the report paths and aggregate counts.
//
// SAFETY NOTES
// - A mapped profile must use IDs present in the supplied DBC/SQL snapshot.
// - Required level and item level are different constraints; do not use one
//   to stand in for the other.
// - Existing loot rows remain additive. Generated cleanup files remove only
//   generated pool/attachment rows.
// - A 20-item legendary recipe is shown in the examples as a planned request,
//   but the current validator still enforces the original approximately
//   3-per-100k legendary policy. Do not uncomment it yet.
{
  "version": 1,
  "profiles": [
    {
      "id": "example_normal",
      "instance": "Example Dungeon",
      "difficulty": "normal",
      "map_id": 578,
      "difficulty_id": 0,
      "item_level_min": 180,
      "item_level_max": 200,
      "additional_drop_chance": 15,
      "encounters": [
        {
          "id": "trash_before_first",
          "kind": "trash",
          "requires": [],
          "weight": 1,
          "targets": [
            {"type": "creature", "entry": 27633}
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "first_boss",
          "kind": "boss",
          "requires": [],
          "weight": 3,
          "targets": [
            {"type": "creature", "entry": 27654}
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "final_boss",
          "kind": "boss",
          "requires": ["first_boss"],
          "weight": 5,
          "quantity": 1,
          "targets": [
            {"type": "creature", "entry": 27656}
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "recipes": [
    {
      "id": "example_epics",
      "count": 10,
      "profile": "example_normal",
      "quality": 4,
      "required_level": [70, 80],
      "item_level": [180, 200],
      "target_kind": "dungeon"
    }
  ],
  "quest_targets": []
}

/*
==========================================================================
COPY/PASTE EXAMPLES - KEEP THESE COMMENTED UNTIL YOU WANT TO USE THEM
==========================================================================

The snippets below are examples, not additional active configuration. Copy a
profile object into the top-level profiles array and a recipe object into the
recipes array. Add a comma after the previous active array item when needed.
The Naxx boss IDs below were read from the repository's current source snapshot.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
1) LEVELS 30-50, USING THE NORMAL ITEM-LEVEL CURVE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

{
  "id": "level_30_50",
  "count": 100,
  "required_level": [30, 50],
  "target_kind": "general"
}

Notes:
- This constrains RequiredLevel only.
- Add "item_level": [50, 90] if the item-level window must also be fixed.
- RequiredLevel and ItemLevel are validated independently.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
2) LEVELS 30-50 WITH EPIC QUALITY AND A FIXED ITEM-LEVEL WINDOW
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

{
  "id": "level_30_50_epics",
  "count": 50,
  "quality": 4,
  "required_level": [30, 50],
  "item_level": [50, 75],
  "target_kind": "general"
}

Notes:
- Quality 4 means Epic.
- A fixed item-level range can make a recipe impossible if it conflicts with
  class, slot, or profile restrictions; validation fails before output.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
